Your turn
5a. Try to resample the bats to a regular 15-minute interval. What survives? 5b. What would you do instead?






NoteSolution






bat_15 &lt;- bat_trk |&gt;
  nest(data = -id) |&gt;
  mutate(data = lapply(data, \(d) d |&gt;
    track_resample(rate = minutes(15), tolerance = minutes(5)) |&gt;
    filter_min_n_burst(min_n = 3))) |&gt;
  unnest(cols = data)

c(before = nrow(bat_trk), after = nrow(bat_15))
#&gt; before  after 
#&gt;   1740    802

bursts &lt;- bat_15 |&gt; count(id, burst_)
c(n_bursts = nrow(bursts), median_length = median(bursts$n), max_length = max(bursts$n))
#&gt;      n_bursts median_length    max_length 
#&gt;           165             4            13
table(bursts$n)
#&gt; 
#&gt;  3  4  5  6  7  8  9 10 11 13 
#&gt; 57 34 26 20  8  7  5  4  2  2

Fewer than half the fixes survive — but the real problem is the shape of what is left. The 802 surviving fixes shatter into 165 bursts with a median length of 4, i.e. three steps each. There is no programmed schedule to recover: fixes arrived whenever a field worker obtained a bearing.
A hidden Markov model (Session 8) estimates transition probabilities between behavioural states. With bursts of three steps, there is almost nothing to transition — you would be asking the model to learn the dynamics of a process from fragments shorter than the process itself.

lags &lt;- bat_trk |&gt;
  group_by(id) |&gt;
  arrange(t_, .by_group = TRUE) |&gt;
  mutate(dt_min = as.numeric(difftime(t_, lag(t_), units = "mins"))) |&gt;
  ungroup() |&gt; filter(!is.na(dt_min), dt_min &gt; 0)

hist(log10(lags$dt_min), breaks = 40, col = "#1f6f78", border = "white",
     xlab = "log10 interval (minutes)", main = "Bat sampling intervals span five orders of magnitude")







What to do instead. Not: force a regular grid and pretend. The honest options are

restrict to the questions these data can answer — which sites were used, broad home-range extent, night-time activity periods;
use methods that tolerate irregular sampling, i.e. continuous-time models (ctmm) rather than discrete-step models;
treat each night as a separate short burst and analyse only within-night movement.

Recognising which of your datasets is a “buffalo” and which is a “bat” is one of the more valuable things you will take from this course.

Task 4 — Daily distance
Straight-line distance between consecutive fixes, summed per day. Because our coordinates are in degrees we need a great-circle distance. Here is the haversine formula written out — Session 2 replaces it with sf, which does this properly on the ellipsoid.

haversine_m &lt;- function(lon1, lat1, lon2, lat2, r = 6371008.8) {
  to_rad &lt;- pi / 180
  dlat &lt;- (lat2 - lat1) * to_rad
  dlon &lt;- (lon2 - lon1) * to_rad
  a &lt;- sin(dlat / 2)^2 + cos(lat1 * to_rad) * cos(lat2 * to_rad) * sin(dlon / 2)^2
  2 * r * asin(sqrt(a))
}

steps &lt;- track |&gt;
  distinct(id, t, .keep_all = TRUE) |&gt;          # drop duplicates first
  group_by(id) |&gt;
  mutate(dist_m = haversine_m(lag(lon), lag(lat), lon, lat),
         dt_h   = as.numeric(difftime(t, lag(t), units = "hours"))) |&gt;
  ungroup() |&gt;
  filter(!is.na(dist_m))

daily &lt;- steps |&gt;
  mutate(day = as_date(t)) |&gt;
  group_by(id, day) |&gt;
  summarise(km = sum(dist_m) / 1000, steps = n(), .groups = "drop")

daily |&gt;
  group_by(id) |&gt;
  summarise(median_km_per_day = round(median(km), 1), .groups = "drop")
#&gt; # A tibble: 6 × 2
#&gt;   id     median_km_per_day
#&gt;   &lt;chr&gt;              &lt;dbl&gt;
#&gt; 1 Cilla                5.9
#&gt; 2 Gabs                 5.3
#&gt; 3 Mvubu                5.6
#&gt; 4 Pepper               4.6
#&gt; 5 Queen                7.3
#&gt; 6 Toni                 5.9

Your turn
4a. Plot daily distance over time for one animal. 4b. Is a day with 6 fixes comparable to a day with 24?






NoteSolution





Queen’s collar was patchy, which makes her the instructive case:

daily |&gt;
  filter(id == "Queen") |&gt;
  ggplot(aes(day, km)) +
  geom_line(colour = "grey70") +
  geom_point(aes(colour = steps &gt;= 22), size = 1.6) +
  scale_colour_manual(values = c(`TRUE` = "#1f6f78", `FALSE` = "firebrick"),
                      labels = c(`TRUE` = "complete day (22+ steps)",
                                 `FALSE` = "incomplete day")) +
  labs(x = NULL, y = "Distance per day (km)", colour = NULL,
       title = "Queen: daily distance") +
  theme_minimal() + theme(legend.position = "bottom")







No, they are not comparable. Summed distance is a lower bound that depends on how many fixes you had — the modifiable temporal unit problem from the slides:

daily |&gt;
  filter(id == "Queen") |&gt;
  mutate(steps_that_day = cut(steps, c(0, 6, 12, 21, Inf),
                              labels = c("1–6", "7–12", "13–21", "22+"))) |&gt;
  group_by(steps_that_day) |&gt;
  summarise(days = n(), median_km = round(median(km), 1))
#&gt; # A tibble: 4 × 3
#&gt;   steps_that_day  days median_km
#&gt;   &lt;fct&gt;          &lt;int&gt;     &lt;dbl&gt;
#&gt; 1 1–6               21       2.5
#&gt; 2 7–12               7       3.7
#&gt; 3 13–21             12       7.3
#&gt; 4 22+              175       7.5

Days with only a handful of fixes come out at a third of the distance of complete days — for a reason that has nothing to do with the buffalo. Before comparing days, keep only complete ones or, better, resample to a regular interval (Session 3).

